WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:05.520
Inside every cell of your body, billions&nbsp;
of tiny machines are hard at work.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:08.123
They carry oxygen in your blood,

00:00:08.123 --> 00:00:10.228
let your eyes&nbsp;detect light 

00:00:10.228 --> 00:00:12.333
and even make your muscles move.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:14.835
These machines are proteins 

00:00:14.835 --> 00:00:18.654
and they underpin&nbsp;every 
biological process in every living thing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:25.018
Each protein has an intricate 3d shape that&nbsp;
defines what it does and how it works. 

00:00:25.104 --> 00:00:28.673
We know of&nbsp;&nbsp;over 200 million proteins 
and counting.  

00:00:28.673 --> 00:00:32.480
But we only&nbsp;know the exact 3D shapes
of a fraction of these.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:35.290
Unravel a protein and you'll see it's like a&nbsp;
string of beads 

00:00:35.290 --> 00:00:38.693
made up of 20 different types&nbsp;
of amino acids.

00:00:38.693 --> 00:00:42.338
 Interactions between these amino&nbsp;acids
make the protein fold   

00:00:42.338 --> 00:00:46.182
as it finds its shape&nbsp;out of almost 
limitless possibilities.   

00:00:46.304 --> 00:00:49.295
For decades a community of 
scientists have been working on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.295 --> 00:00:53.862
way to figure out a protein shape 
just from its&nbsp;string of amino acids. 

00:00:53.862 --> 00:00:57.081
This is a major challenge&nbsp;in our
understanding of biology

00:00:57.081 --> 00:01:00.564
So, we created an&nbsp;AI system
to solve this problem... &nbsp;

00:01:00.564 --> 00:01:02.240
called AlphaFold.&nbsp;

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:06.649
It's trained on the sequences and structures of&nbsp;
about a hundred thousand proteins 

00:01:06.649 --> 00:01:09.879
painstakingly&nbsp;&nbsp;mapped out 
by scientists around the world.

00:01:09.879 --> 00:01:12.880
Today it can accurately predict 
a protein's shape...&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:15.533
just from its sequence of amino acids. &nbsp;

00:01:15.533 --> 00:01:19.868
AlphaFold's predictions could 
enable progress in&nbsp;all sorts of areas.

00:01:19.868 --> 00:01:23.200
Imagine a future where&nbsp;we 
can understand diseases more quickly...&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:25.452
and develop drugs to fight them.

00:01:25.452 --> 00:01:28.584
Or one where&nbsp;we could use enzymes
to break down plastic&nbsp;waste...

00:01:28.584 --> 00:01:31.706
or even to capture carbon from the&nbsp;
atmosphere.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.706 --> 00:01:33.663
All with the help of proteins.

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:36.916
There's a lot more work to be done 

00:01:36.916 --> 00:01:39.280
but unlocking&nbsp;the shapes 
of these building blocks &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:42.669
could help scientists 
better understand the natural&nbsp;world...

00:01:42.669 --> 00:01:45.440
and perhaps expand our 
knowledge of life itself.

